00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:22.520
OK, so the organization of the talk today
is broken down into just basically five parts.

00:02:22.520 --> 00:02:26.520
I may have too
much material so I'll probably start skipping

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:30.730
stuff if we-- if it looks as though we're
running short of time.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:32.550
But
essentially, I'm going to try and just give

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:36.430
a high level overview as to what scientific
knowledge engineering is really

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:37.900
all about.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:44.440
I'm going to describe how this kind of works
currently in the community, how people do

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:48.630
knowledge
systems and how scientific databases and scientific

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.590
databases leverage the kind of technology
that scientific

00:02:51.590 --> 00:02:54.530
knowledge engineering is all about.

00:02:54.530 --> 00:03:02.500
But I also want to highlight some failings
and questiond about general

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:06.110
approach that people have towards scientific
knowledge engineering in that most people,

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:09.010
when they think of this
discipline, they think of knowledge schemas

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:10.220
and database schemas.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:11.470
And that's not the whole thing.

00:03:11.470 --> 00:03:16.210
Then, I'm just, at the end of the talk, if
we've got time, I'm going to talk a little

00:03:16.210 --> 00:03:21.980
bit about my own ideas and about
how I see the world and strategies that I

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:26.850
think can help people who are trying to develop
their own systems, you

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:32.390
know, how you could go about starting the
process of doing that and what are some important

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:34.770
things to think
about when you're actually kind of getting

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.950
started with building a system.

00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:42.820
And I wanted to start-- end with a kind of
high level idea of just presenting the kind

00:03:42.820 --> 00:03:45.640
of notion of artificial scientific
intelligence.

00:03:45.640 --> 00:03:50.840
What would it look like to have machines be
able to do science?

00:03:50.840 --> 00:03:55.310
OK, so first question is, what is scientific
knowledge engineering?

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:57.810
And I wanted to kind of draw on a book by
Dr.

00:03:57.810 --> 00:04:02.410
Paul Rosenblum who wrote a book on the great
scientific domains.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:08.300
And he actually was talking about computing
as a great scientific domain in the same way

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.700
that physical sciences, life sciences, and
social sciences are.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.690
He kind
of draws a nice idea that says, well, you

00:04:14.690 --> 00:04:17.730
know, you need-- when we think of science,
we think of social science,

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:19.020
physical science, and life science.

00:04:19.020 --> 00:04:26.760
But computer science is a valid kind of companion
of these three, but it also

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:30.610
beautifully kind of complements them and works
together with them.

00:04:30.610 --> 00:04:34.990
And the definition that he has for computing
is one I really liked, which simply says that

00:04:34.990 --> 00:04:39.250
computing involves
transformation of information.

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:46.470
And when we're thinking about the evolution
of science, we go invariably to the

00:04:46.470 --> 00:04:52.340
great Jim Gray from Microsoft who-- and if
anybody who's listening kind of wants a good

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:55.420
time, they should go to
this website at the bottom to look at some

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:57.070
of Jim's talks.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:04:58.820
He was a luminary.

00:04:58.820 --> 00:05:03.900
He's sadly no longer with us, but
really fantastic work.

00:05:03.900 --> 00:05:10.850
And his vision was that whereas thousands
of years ago, people would perform empirical

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:11.850
science.

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.310
So you
basically look at the stars and you try to

00:05:13.310 --> 00:05:14.500
explain them.

00:05:14.500 --> 00:05:18.010
And then as we progressed, we would develop
analytical

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:20.080
approaches to kind of explaining things.

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:24.270
You build analytical models to describe the
physics of the motion of

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.220
planets and such like.

00:05:26.220 --> 00:05:31.600
And then more recently, we've gotten into
computational science where all of a sudden,

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:35.260
we have large scale data
sets and large scale analytical models.

00:05:35.260 --> 00:05:39.310
And we're able to generate computation on
these things.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:42.370
And then the
final, the kind of fourth paradigm as he talks

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:47.070
about is this notion of e-science where you
have a lot of data, huge

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:51.650
amount of data big data captured by instruments
generated by simulators and then processed

00:05:51.650 --> 00:05:56.150
by high power,
high power tools such as workflows and then

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:59.520
can be placed into a database, a file, a set
of files or a knowledge

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:00.520
base.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:04.120
And then the scientist kind of interacts with
the database on those files in order to make

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:07.020
predictions and do their
scientific work.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:11.960
And although, you know, I think this is a
beautiful representation of how things have

00:06:11.960 --> 00:06:14.889
evolved, we've
certainly not gotten rid of empirical science

00:06:14.889 --> 00:06:16.420
or analytic science.

00:06:16.420 --> 00:06:18.449
So it's worth thinking that even though we
have

00:06:18.449 --> 00:06:23.370
evolved to include e-science in the whole
process, we still need to think about empirical

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:28.770
science as a process that
is still valid and still important on certain

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:29.770
things.

00:06:29.770 --> 00:06:34.400
And so, you know, the point that I'm making
here is really the competing, in other words,

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:39.300
the notion that the
transformation of information has always been

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:40.540
fundamental to the scientific work.

00:06:40.540 --> 00:06:43.890
And whenever we've done
science in the past or in the future, we are

00:06:43.890 --> 00:06:46.940
always going to have to have structures that
allow us to deal with

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:49.110
information, transform it, and make sense
of it.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:50.110
Praise it.

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:52.230
So before computers we had scientific notebooks.

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:56.810
This is
a page from Darwin's field notebook.

00:06:56.810 --> 00:06:58.830
We have libraries, literature.

00:06:58.830 --> 00:07:02.560
We have mathematics, scientific theory, classical
statistics, all of these structures that help

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:07.290
us manipulate
information not on a computer, but, nonetheless,

00:07:07.290 --> 00:07:09.160
we're still manipulating information.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:13.360
And now of course, we have
large scale data analysis methods and simulation

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:15.520
tools and databases to help us with this process.

00:07:15.520 --> 00:07:19.160
But we
shouldn't loose track of the idea that, essentially,

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:23.130
computation, computing is kind of a fundamental
aspect of

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:25.990
scientific work and has been from the very
start.

00:07:25.990 --> 00:07:31.620
Now just to kind of dive into a little bit
of philosophy, if you'll forgive me, I wanted

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:34.150
to introduce the term reification.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:39.669
And reification is an interesting kind of
idea that basically, it's when we try to think

00:07:39.669 --> 00:07:45.400
of something abstract like a
concept or an idea as a material or concrete

00:07:45.400 --> 00:07:46.400
thing.

00:07:46.400 --> 00:07:49.520
And so what's interesting about this is that
we can think of

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:54.310
apparatus-- so this is a quote from a book
called Laboratory Life, which is, again, a

00:07:54.310 --> 00:07:55.310
great read.

00:07:55.310 --> 00:07:59.360
It just says, it's
like, we can think of, "apparatus as 'reified'

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:00.360
theory.

00:08:00.360 --> 00:08:02.820
So when another member uses the NMR spectrometer,"
for

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:07.450
example, "to check the purity of his compound,
he is using spin theory and the outcome of

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:08.840
20 years of basic
physics research."

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:14.460
So in other words, the equipment and the tools
and the systems that we build somehow kind

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:21.290
of concretize
abstract scientific theory into a thing, into

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:22.800
objects.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.750
And those objects can be virtual in the sense
they can exist as

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:28.759
algorithms or computer programs, live in a
computer.

00:08:28.759 --> 00:08:32.510
Or they can be actual physical apparatus like
an NMR

00:08:32.510 --> 00:08:33.510
spectrometer.

00:08:33.510 --> 00:08:35.969
And so that's really what this is about.

00:08:35.969 --> 00:08:40.110
Scientific knowledge engineering is really
about, how do you turn scientific

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:44.830
knowledge into a material or concrete thing?

00:08:44.830 --> 00:08:49.320
And the process of basically developing information-based
systems

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:54.500
to capture, store, and use scientific knowledge
is central to this whole idea.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:59.000
And so, you know, if you were to try
and turn around and boil down the essence

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:02.190
of what scientific knowledge engineering is
really about as a

00:09:02.190 --> 00:09:06.880
fundamental challenge, essentially, we are
asking the questions, OK, how do you build

00:09:06.880 --> 00:09:09.120
a representation of
scientific knowledge?

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:11.380
In other words, how do you write it down?

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:14.250
How do you write the scientific knowledge
down into a structure that you

00:09:14.250 --> 00:09:18.390
then would process and use with computers
and such.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:23.399
And so at that, that's the kind of high level
introduction to

00:09:23.399 --> 00:09:26.000
scientific knowledge engineering is, in my
mind.

00:09:26.000 --> 00:09:30.310
And so let's go into a little bit of how this
has shown up in

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:36.120
computational systems and things that we have
access to in the world.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:44.100
So this is taken from a paper which is a nice
recent review of scientific knowledge engineering

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:45.100
I'd recommend.

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:50.650
And all the way through the talk, I'll be
citing information from other sources.

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:56.540
And hopefully the review-- and I'll
include references so that anyone watching

00:09:56.540 --> 00:10:01.080
or reading this can see where the information
comes from.

00:10:01.080 --> 00:10:04.070
By all
means, please follow up on these links and

00:10:04.070 --> 00:10:05.720
see if you can find things.

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:12.120
OK, so Dos Santos and Travassos in their review
describe the kind of basic fundamental idea

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:15.960
of what scientific
knowledge engineering is when basically, we're

00:10:15.960 --> 00:10:18.440
trying to build computational infrstructure.

00:10:18.440 --> 00:10:20.880
This is the little block
that you see on the right hand side.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:24.110
And we have scientists engaging with the machine
in two ways.

00:10:24.110 --> 00:10:28.320
First of all, as a knowledge engineer-- in
other words, you're basically trying to build

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:32.770
the underlying schema and
infrastructure of the system to of make it

00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:33.770
work.

00:10:33.770 --> 00:10:36.720
And then the scientist himself interacts with
the system to make

00:10:36.720 --> 00:10:40.680
inquiries, to ask questions, and to get answers
back.

00:10:40.680 --> 00:10:44.610
And essentially, that's a good way of thinking
about the core

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:49.300
kind of role and approach to scientific knowledge
and knowledge engineering has.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:57.850
And so at this point, it's kind of fun to
think of the very first biomedical database

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:02.240
that was ever created by humans
in a real, concrete way.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:05.350
And this is, of course, the Protein Databank
or PDB.

00:11:05.350 --> 00:11:10.550
And this originated at a Cold Spring
Harbor Symposium in 1972.

00:11:10.550 --> 00:11:17.980
And it really kind of came about because technology
for getting hold of molecular

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:21.580
structural data was becoming more and more
common.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:26.370
And not only that, but you were able to actually
display and showcase your computer programs

00:11:26.370 --> 00:11:30.600
being created at
that time, very expensive and impressive computer

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:35.080
programs, that were able to render the images
of these 3D

00:11:35.080 --> 00:11:38.310
models and kind of display them for a user
to be able to see.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:41.810
So that's revolutionary at the time.

00:11:41.810 --> 00:11:44.510
And basically,
what was it was interesting about this was

00:11:44.510 --> 00:11:48.680
that every single molecule representation
consisted of a bunch of

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:53.880
different 3-D positions of atoms in 3-D space.

00:11:53.880 --> 00:11:58.360
And up until this point, no one had any real,
concrete way of being

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:00.840
able to share the information within the community.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:05.600
So it's a small group of people, all of whom
are experts.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:07.709
But they need to be able to share data.

00:12:07.709 --> 00:12:13.000
And so from-- and
so they basically organized this transatlantic

00:12:13.000 --> 00:12:17.320
collaboration between America and Great Britain
and were able to

00:12:17.320 --> 00:12:24.390
generate two of these that has, since 1971,
has clearly really had an impact in the world

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:30.649
and kind of has, still has
very, very significant information and has

00:12:30.649 --> 00:12:34.870
embraced, you know, has really motivated research
at the highest

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:36.620
possible level.

00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:40.769
And so this is the kind of example of the
kind of data, the kind of systems that we're

00:12:40.769 --> 00:12:43.090
talking about,
the kind of things that we're talking about.

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:52.100
Now, if we fast forward to 2017, there is
a data-- there is a publication called Nucleic

00:12:52.100 --> 00:13:01.430
Acids Research who every
year publish a single article-- oh, sorry,

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:07.870
a single issue that is devoted to all of the
molecular biology databases that

00:13:07.870 --> 00:13:10.959
you can possibly think of in the world.

00:13:10.959 --> 00:13:14.680
And every year, they basically receive papers
that describe these

00:13:14.680 --> 00:13:15.690
databases.

00:13:15.690 --> 00:13:21.000
So each paper is a full write-up of each database
in detail.

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:31.910
And every year, as you can see, they add about
200 or 300 individual databases to their repository.

00:13:31.910 --> 00:13:36.910
And since
they've started-- this is now in its 24th

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:40.450
year-- since they've started, the collection
of databases that they have

00:13:40.450 --> 00:13:47.829
amassed in terms of the descriptions that
they have consists of 15 categories, 41 subcategories,

00:13:47.829 --> 00:13:53.180
and there's
almost 2,000-- 1,893 separate databases listed

00:13:53.180 --> 00:13:55.060
in that collection.

00:13:55.060 --> 00:14:01.930
And it's worth noting that 110 of these systems
that they describe are really of the highest

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:02.930
possible quality.

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:05.920
These
are the so-called golden set of databases

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:09.930
that consist of the kind of systems that pretty
much all biologists in the

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:16.920
world use, so things like UniProt and IEBB,
these very, very high quality systems that

00:14:16.920 --> 00:14:19.160
are relied upon within the
community.

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:22.700
Now it's worth noting as well that these are
all molecular scientific databases.

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:28.589
So it is a subset of the
systems-- of the types of systems that are

00:14:28.589 --> 00:14:29.589
available.

00:14:29.589 --> 00:14:36.210
And not only that, but they are-- the data
that goes into these databases tends to be

00:14:36.210 --> 00:14:38.540
more quantitative and more
tractable.

00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:43.370
So even though it's complicated, even though
there's a lot of, huge number of different

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:45.769
dimensions of
this information that we need to track and

00:14:45.769 --> 00:14:51.920
keep and moderate, it's actually, I would
say, at some level

00:14:51.920 --> 00:14:57.709
conceptually-- excuse me-- conceptually simple
and relatively easy to kind of capture.

00:14:57.709 --> 00:15:01.820
Although, of course, it's still
very difficult.

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:03.590
But it is possible.

00:15:03.590 --> 00:15:11.160
So the state of the art for scientific databases
currently is that the kind of technology that

00:15:11.160 --> 00:15:18.860
goes into generating
these systems, all 1,893 of them, is simply

00:15:18.860 --> 00:15:22.500
normal data-driven web applications.

00:15:22.500 --> 00:15:26.440
These are databases that serve
scientific communities, but the chances are

00:15:26.440 --> 00:15:30.720
the technology that underlies them is kind
of similar to the types of

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:37.709
websites that you might see online from any
kind of industry or any kind of company.

00:15:37.709 --> 00:15:43.589
If anything, they probably lag
somewhat behind the curve in terms of the

00:15:43.589 --> 00:15:49.079
sophistication of the portal interfaces and
such like because we are on

00:15:49.079 --> 00:15:53.790
scientific budgets and we have to get money
from the government to do this rather than

00:15:53.790 --> 00:15:58.800
being able to hire, being
able to command the kind of salaries that

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:02.510
Google and Facebook and these other places
are able to do.

00:16:02.510 --> 00:16:06.540
But nonetheless, the technology that underlies
it is pretty standard.

00:16:06.540 --> 00:16:08.970
Usually, it's something like a website with
a

00:16:08.970 --> 00:16:13.740
relational database back-end implemented either
in a commercial systems like Oracle, MySQL,

00:16:13.740 --> 00:16:15.760
PostgreS-- or
anything like that.

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:21.079
And so within this context, what scientific
knowledge engineering really is all about

00:16:21.079 --> 00:16:24.810
here is really
how do you design a database and how do you

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:27.440
design a web application.

00:16:27.440 --> 00:16:31.450
And of course, the interesting challenge of
that is that rather than dealing with information

00:16:31.450 --> 00:16:34.700
that you will find in
Facebook or a business company or something

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:37.160
like that, you actually have to deal with
complex scientific

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:38.160
knowledge.

00:16:38.160 --> 00:16:40.730
And that is, itself, of course, a challenge.

00:16:40.730 --> 00:16:43.889
Because scientific knowledge is hard to understand.

00:16:43.889 --> 00:16:44.889
It's
complicated.

00:16:44.889 --> 00:16:45.889
It's multifaceted.

00:16:45.889 --> 00:16:48.940
It has a whole bunch of different things going
for it.

00:16:48.940 --> 00:16:53.190
So, essentially, when we're talking about
these, all these hundreds and thousands of

00:16:53.190 --> 00:16:56.589
databases that exist online,
essentially the question we have to ask from

00:16:56.589 --> 00:16:59.250
a scientific knowledge engineering standpoint
is, how do you

00:16:59.250 --> 00:17:00.500
structure your data?

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:03.600
How do you understand how to put this together?

00:17:03.600 --> 00:17:07.490
So first question you have to ask is, how
should your database schema describe the data

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:09.050
you want to share?

00:17:09.050 --> 00:17:14.010
The next question that you'd have to ask is,
well, where does the data come from you want

00:17:14.010 --> 00:17:15.459
to put in your
database?

00:17:15.459 --> 00:17:18.429
Now, this can come from the literature.

00:17:18.429 --> 00:17:21.720
And if you do derive it from the scientific
literature, maybe you

00:17:21.720 --> 00:17:23.920
need to hire people to read the literature.

00:17:23.920 --> 00:17:28.850
In other words, these are called curators
of curation style who are

00:17:28.850 --> 00:17:34.320
typically expert scientists themselves and,
therefore, are quite expensive to pay and

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:35.440
to maintain.

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:39.490
But nonetheless,
in order to be able to actually preserve a

00:17:39.490 --> 00:17:44.130
half-decent database, you need to have high
quality, high quality curated

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:45.290
information.

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:51.330
Or maybe you're-- maybe the database that
you're working with wants to store laboratory-based

00:17:51.330 --> 00:17:55.570
information that
comes, that's used, that isn't published that

00:17:55.570 --> 00:17:59.190
is coming off of a machine somewhere.

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:03.700
And if that's the case, then
you have to consider how data is formatted.

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:05.000
And also, what about metadata?

00:18:05.000 --> 00:18:08.179
What about the context of the-- of
the data itself?

00:18:08.179 --> 00:18:15.169
So if you have-- let's say that you have an
MRI database that you, you want to store magnetic

00:18:15.169 --> 00:18:20.040
resonance
imagings from, you are going to have to actually--

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:24.059
you can't just take all of the files that
you have, put them online,

00:18:24.059 --> 00:18:26.020
and say, hey, everybody go at it.

00:18:26.020 --> 00:18:27.049
Take a look at this.

00:18:27.049 --> 00:18:31.070
You have to think about how the data itself
is structured that

00:18:31.070 --> 00:18:35.400
describes each individual person that the
scans are taken from and what the context

00:18:35.400 --> 00:18:37.250
and how it all works.

00:18:37.250 --> 00:18:39.360
That is itself a very big challenge too.

00:18:39.360 --> 00:18:42.900
In fact, that's probably the bigger challenge
than storing the original data

00:18:42.900 --> 00:18:43.910
files.

00:18:43.910 --> 00:18:50.740
And of course, it's like the other question
is, how should your website serve the data

00:18:50.740 --> 00:18:51.740
to your end users?

00:18:51.740 --> 00:18:53.549
So
you have a bunch of people who need access

00:18:53.549 --> 00:18:55.090
to this information.

00:18:55.090 --> 00:18:57.540
What's the context of the access that they're
looking for?

00:18:57.540 --> 00:19:00.850
How do you-- what kind of functionality do
you provide?

00:19:00.850 --> 00:19:04.390
All of these questions are actually scientific
knowledge engineering questions and require

00:19:04.390 --> 00:19:09.620
you to think about and
construct the system pretty much in the same

00:19:09.620 --> 00:19:12.980
way that you would do for any other web application
or any other

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:14.580
web system.

00:19:14.580 --> 00:19:18.860
But because it's scientific data, you have
to think about these things in a more deep

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:19.860
way.

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:24.929
And then, finally, a question that comes up
that's important for people in the community

00:19:24.929 --> 00:19:27.880
is, how are you handling
standardization of your information?

00:19:27.880 --> 00:19:35.720
So, as you can see, if we have 1,893 databases,
they can't all use different

00:19:35.720 --> 00:19:37.800
ways of describing what a protein is.

00:19:37.800 --> 00:19:40.040
They need to standardize the approach that
they're using.

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:42.980
And there are-- as
we'll get into it, this is actually probably

00:19:42.980 --> 00:19:46.700
one of the key questions of scientific knowledge
engineering that we're

00:19:46.700 --> 00:19:48.590
going to cover today.

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:56.760
But, in fact, it's certainly not an easy--
not an easy thing to kind of address.

00:19:56.760 --> 00:20:05.049
So the question then comes into, at what stage
in the scientific process do you actually

00:20:05.049 --> 00:20:07.120
build your system?

00:20:07.120 --> 00:20:09.750
This
diagram very quickly kind of shows the various

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:13.679
different types of options that you have.

00:20:13.679 --> 00:20:18.669
And there are people who
build systems straight from the laboratory.

00:20:18.669 --> 00:20:24.460
My colleague Karl Castleman at ISI has built
a system called Deriva that does just this.

00:20:24.460 --> 00:20:29.260
And then perhaps you-- or
you might want to build a system that describes

00:20:29.260 --> 00:20:33.970
the primary literature, the literature that
kind of captures the

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:36.039
experimental data at source.

00:20:36.039 --> 00:20:40.450
Or maybe you want to build a database that
captures and summarizes the review

00:20:40.450 --> 00:20:41.710
literature.

00:20:41.710 --> 00:20:47.010
Each one of these has has a various different
set of parameters and balances and checks

00:20:47.010 --> 00:20:48.470
that needs to
happen.

00:20:48.470 --> 00:20:53.220
But it's interesting that the further away
you get from the laboratory, the more kind

00:20:53.220 --> 00:20:59.600
of, well, the less
efficient the system is and the more kind

00:20:59.600 --> 00:21:03.970
of pre-chewed that the data can be.

00:21:03.970 --> 00:21:07.760
It's actually-- and, of course, this efficiency
question is pretty important.

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:17.070
It seems silly for us to build, for us to
spend huge amounts of money on funding to

00:21:17.070 --> 00:21:21.080
scientists to do their work, publish papers,
and then have to spend

00:21:21.080 --> 00:21:24.960
huge amounts of money on funding to develop
ways of extracting that information from the

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:28.120
primary literature in
order to put it to databases, which is actually

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:31.850
kind of what we're doing at the moment.

00:21:31.850 --> 00:21:41.020
So-- OK, so, I'm going back to standardization,
this is a comic from XKCB, which I think captures

00:21:41.020 --> 00:21:52.270
very well how this
process, sadly, does actually work.

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:57.000
If you have a situation where there are many
competing standards, as is the

00:21:57.000 --> 00:22:03.860
case with biomedical databases and all of
these various different approaches, if you

00:22:03.860 --> 00:22:07.880
come into the situation say,
oh my god, there's 14 competing standards,

00:22:07.880 --> 00:22:10.470
we need to develop one universal standard
that covers everybody's

00:22:10.470 --> 00:22:14.220
use cases, and everybody agrees with you and
this is great, then typically, what happens

00:22:14.220 --> 00:22:17.600
is your standard
becomes yet another example of a competing

00:22:17.600 --> 00:22:20.860
standard that has to compete with everything
else.

00:22:20.860 --> 00:22:24.860
Now, this is a
pitfall that a lot of people are aware of

00:22:24.860 --> 00:22:29.320
and are trying desperately to kind of work
around, but, nonetheless, as

00:22:29.320 --> 00:22:36.890
you'll probably see quite soon, this is still
a real problem that causes headaches for scientists

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:40.809
all the time.

00:22:40.809 --> 00:22:48.049
Now, so I just wanted to talk very briefly
about-- just to give you kind of-- just to

00:22:48.049 --> 00:22:55.530
give everybody on the call an idea
of the complexity of what a database typical,

00:22:55.530 --> 00:22:57.900
good quality database schema would look like.

00:22:57.900 --> 00:23:02.350
So there's a system
called CHADO or CHADO-- I'm not sure if that's

00:23:02.350 --> 00:23:05.650
the correct pronunciation-- which is a part
of the Generic Model

00:23:05.650 --> 00:23:08.480
Organism Database project.

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:11.840
This is a database that's implemented in PostgreSQL.

00:23:11.840 --> 00:23:16.410
And essentially, it's designed
to provide standardized schema-- I'm sorry,

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:22.590
have a for Genomic data associated with generic
model organisms.

00:23:22.590 --> 00:23:28.919
Now genetic model organisms-- organisms are
things like mice, safflower, fruit flies,

00:23:28.919 --> 00:23:36.350
cats, rats, anything, any kind
of standard organism that people use to perform

00:23:36.350 --> 00:23:38.809
experiments on.

00:23:38.809 --> 00:23:43.870
And one of the kind of goals of the GMOD
project is to standardize representational

00:23:43.870 --> 00:23:47.480
processing of data-- of different types from
these various different model

00:23:47.480 --> 00:23:51.030
organisms in a standard way.

00:23:51.030 --> 00:23:53.660
The database itself is actually quite big.

00:23:53.660 --> 00:24:01.030
It consists of 18 so-called modules, which
subdivide the task into 133 tables.

00:24:01.030 --> 00:24:04.020
But it is used by a relatively large number
of databases for different model

00:24:04.020 --> 00:24:05.020
organisms.

00:24:05.020 --> 00:24:10.100
And so it's a-- you can consider this to be
a success in the community.

00:24:10.100 --> 00:24:15.679
And I just wanted to kind of show you that
the basic data structure of these things can

00:24:15.679 --> 00:24:17.020
actually get quite
complicated.

00:24:17.020 --> 00:24:24.480
So one of the tables is this notion of a feature
in the database.

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:31.240
This is the kind of thing that you might
do that would be a general kind of, a general

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:43.330
purpose phenotypic aspect of model organisms
process or, you

00:24:43.330 --> 00:24:47.409
know, something that's happening in the model
organism that you want to keep track of.

00:24:47.409 --> 00:24:51.320
And if you draw out all
data tables associated with this-- including

00:24:51.320 --> 00:24:56.929
links to the organism, links to publications
in the pub table, or locations

00:24:56.929 --> 00:25:06.590
of the features-- this is actually quite a
fairly complicated and in-depth data structure.

00:25:06.590 --> 00:25:07.760
Understanding this is no joke.

00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:16.409
And of course, if you look at, in fact, if
you look at the structure of the database

00:25:16.409 --> 00:25:17.409
as a
whole.

00:25:17.409 --> 00:25:21.559
If you look at the scaled up for the whole
thing, it would be vastly more complicated.

00:25:21.559 --> 00:25:24.409
And this is actually
quite difficult to understand.

00:25:24.409 --> 00:25:30.110
In addition what's important is that the semantics
and the structure of the database is

00:25:30.110 --> 00:25:35.620
often kind of embedded in the names of the
actual terms that occur.

00:25:35.620 --> 00:25:40.049
So the CV term is a table for a controlled
vocabulary term.

00:25:40.049 --> 00:25:45.960
And the nature of the semantics for the database
are going to depend very heavily on the types

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:48.280
of terms that go
into that table.

00:25:48.280 --> 00:25:52.460
So from a scientific knowledge engineering
point of view, being able to understand and

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:57.900
build this
kind of system, you have the underlying infrastructure

00:25:57.900 --> 00:26:00.370
of the database tables that you have to understand
if you're

00:26:00.370 --> 00:26:02.470
working with GMOD databases.

00:26:02.470 --> 00:26:06.470
But you also have to understand the terminology
and you have to understand

00:26:06.470 --> 00:26:12.539
how the various different terms linked together
and link to existing databases and to all

00:26:12.539 --> 00:26:13.539
these things.

00:26:13.539 --> 00:26:17.160
So this is just to kind of give you a glimpse
of the level of complexity that's required

00:26:17.160 --> 00:26:20.750
in these various different
approaches and systems.

00:26:20.750 --> 00:26:28.350
OK, so in order to kind of deal with this
notion of standardization across the various

00:26:28.350 --> 00:26:33.690
different biomedical databases that we will
encounter, people in the community have developed

00:26:33.690 --> 00:26:39.260
this-- developed
this notion or rather used the notion of ontologies.

00:26:39.260 --> 00:26:44.470
And ontologies is a construct from AI, which
is, the best

00:26:44.470 --> 00:26:47.850
definition I've come across is that by Gruber
in 1993.

00:26:47.850 --> 00:26:51.680
He says simply that it is a specification
of a

00:26:51.680 --> 00:26:52.680
conceptualization.

00:26:52.680 --> 00:26:57.690
So in other words, the way in which it's just
a kind of a representation, computational

00:26:57.690 --> 00:27:00.409
representation of how concepts come about.

00:27:00.409 --> 00:27:09.530
And my colleague Ed Hovy in 2005 wrote a beautiful
little chapter that discusses five methods

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:10.530
of ontology
construction.

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:16.250
And he breaks it down from a high-- from a
very high level looking at the various different

00:27:16.250 --> 00:27:21.520
communities of people who build ontologies
within the computer science community-- within

00:27:21.520 --> 00:27:23.700
the computer science
community.

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:26.610
And he describes them as five different types.

00:27:26.610 --> 00:27:31.720
So there's the philosophers, the cognitive
scientists, the linguists, computational reasoners,

00:27:31.720 --> 00:27:33.750
and the domain
specialists.

00:27:33.750 --> 00:27:38.890
And what's interesting about this is that
typically-- and we'll go into a little bit

00:27:38.890 --> 00:27:45.640
of how biomedical
ontologies are largely driven by a philosopher's

00:27:45.640 --> 00:27:50.520
perspective currently-- but there are other
approaches that are

00:27:50.520 --> 00:27:55.840
based upon, which we won't touch on in detail,
but there are other approaches derived from

00:27:55.840 --> 00:27:59.500
linguistic approaches
using just the meanings of words or domain

00:27:59.500 --> 00:28:03.630
specialists, such as biomedical scientists,
who just need to kind of

00:28:03.630 --> 00:28:08.490
represent the information in their community
as accurately and straightfowardly as possible.

00:28:08.490 --> 00:28:11.870
They typically have a
different approach than philosophers.

00:28:11.870 --> 00:28:15.880
But it's worth kind of trying to consider
all of them.

00:28:15.880 --> 00:28:20.690
And I think the thing that I wanted to leave
everybody on the call with, if-- just as a

00:28:20.690 --> 00:28:26.720
kind of a real, concrete piece of
advice-- is that if anyone tells you that

00:28:26.720 --> 00:28:30.120
there's one, only one correct way of developing
ontology, there's a

00:28:30.120 --> 00:28:35.360
standard methodology that everybody uses,
that everyone agrees on, they're wrong.

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:38.230
This thing doesn't-- there
isn't such a thing.

00:28:38.230 --> 00:28:41.950
There are schools of thought and there are
people who address the question in a given

00:28:41.950 --> 00:28:44.500
way,
but there's no one correct way.

00:28:44.500 --> 00:28:51.980
I think that it would be-- do a disservice
to anyone to try and suggest that only one

00:28:51.980 --> 00:28:53.990
is that the best way.

00:28:53.990 --> 00:28:56.480
And of
course, different types of methodologies trying

00:28:56.480 --> 00:28:59.549
to address this question run into different
pitfalls and problems.

00:28:59.549 --> 00:29:02.159
So
if we're going to solve the problem appropriately

00:29:02.159 --> 00:29:06.660
and powerfully, we need to use all tools at
our disposal to kind of

00:29:06.660 --> 00:29:12.279
try and make sense of things by leveraging
any and all approaches that work and provide

00:29:12.279 --> 00:29:15.130
us with actual working
tools.

00:29:15.130 --> 00:29:24.950
OK, so I wanted to talk about the very important
set of work from the scientific community

00:29:24.950 --> 00:29:28.060
called the Open
Biomedical Ontologies, actually.

00:29:28.060 --> 00:29:31.410
And they call themselves the OBO Foundry.

00:29:31.410 --> 00:29:37.029
And this is an attempt to standardize
ontologies across all of biology, to provide

00:29:37.029 --> 00:29:39.490
a kind of common framework for building these
knowledge

00:29:39.490 --> 00:29:42.960
representations that everybody can use in
the same way.

00:29:42.960 --> 00:29:48.470
This approach doesn't solve many important
standard ontologies such as the gene ontology,

00:29:48.470 --> 00:29:52.840
KB human disease
ontology, there's one called the ontology

00:29:52.840 --> 00:29:56.010
of medical investigation, OB, that's significant.

00:29:56.010 --> 00:29:59.090
And so there are a bunch
of these various different representations

00:29:59.090 --> 00:30:04.600
of knowledge that we-- that you should be
aware of.

00:30:04.600 --> 00:30:08.740
And the approach
broadly uses the basic form of ontology as

00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:10.370
an [INAUDIBLE] ontology.

00:30:10.370 --> 00:30:15.370
And we'll describe very briefly what basic
formal ontology is all about, just to give

00:30:15.370 --> 00:30:20.500
you an idea of how it all is
structured.

00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:25.940
And so OBO Foundry lists 156 separate ontologies.

00:30:25.940 --> 00:30:28.900
And this is very much driven by a community
of

00:30:28.900 --> 00:30:36.860
philosophers who have a kind of a clear idea
as to how they recommend seeing the world.

00:30:36.860 --> 00:30:40.690
And part of the
representation, as you'll see, it tries to

00:30:40.690 --> 00:30:46.800
provide a kind of global catch-all type of
view of the world, of the things that

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:51.539
exist in the world so that we can all kind
of fit our various different-- if you want

00:30:51.539 --> 00:30:58.289
to build a representation of things
from domain A, then that should be able to

00:30:58.289 --> 00:31:02.580
fit into the schema provided by the-- the
Basic Formal Ontology.

00:31:02.580 --> 00:31:06.950
If you
want to look at other things, they should

00:31:06.950 --> 00:31:07.950
all fit together.

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:10.760
This is the kind of vision of the OBO community,
how they

00:31:10.760 --> 00:31:12.220
would like to see it.

00:31:12.220 --> 00:31:14.620
And this is a complicated diagram.

00:31:14.620 --> 00:31:15.890
Don't panic.

00:31:15.890 --> 00:31:17.340
I'm going to kind of walk through it.

00:31:17.340 --> 00:31:23.309
But this is the kind of high level
representation of how OBO Foundry ontologies

00:31:23.309 --> 00:31:25.260
are constructed.

00:31:25.260 --> 00:31:28.899
The first, the top level object is just an
idea, it's a

00:31:28.899 --> 00:31:29.899
theme.

00:31:29.899 --> 00:31:34.880
And then there are two types of underlying--
then they basically split the universe into

00:31:34.880 --> 00:31:38.070
two different types,
into continuance and occurrence.

00:31:38.070 --> 00:31:43.630
And so the idea is just like when you talk
about a thing and then the basic way-- I'm

00:31:43.630 --> 00:31:47.250
not going to go into too much
detail about how all these various different

00:31:47.250 --> 00:31:52.789
elements work-- and I hope I get this correct--
but occurrence are

00:31:52.789 --> 00:31:57.070
things that do not physically exist in the
world.

00:31:57.070 --> 00:31:58.850
Continuance exists in the world as physical
objects.

00:31:58.850 --> 00:32:02.039
So over here,
you see on the left hand side there's an entity

00:32:02.039 --> 00:32:03.730
called the material entity.

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:10.770
And that could be a supernova or a
neutron star or a cup or a chair or a pen

00:32:10.770 --> 00:32:12.120
or a neuron, right?

00:32:12.120 --> 00:32:15.280
Those are all physical objects.

00:32:15.280 --> 00:32:20.960
And, essentially, the
way in which-- the way in which the BFO represents

00:32:20.960 --> 00:32:25.610
this is they kind of-- they try and construct
things that exist in

00:32:25.610 --> 00:32:26.820
the world.

00:32:26.820 --> 00:32:31.419
And they distinguish between the different
things in order to kind of construct a kind

00:32:31.419 --> 00:32:37.750
of global theory of how things
are organized.

00:32:37.750 --> 00:32:45.370
So, for example, a spatial region like your,
I don't know, country of, the United States

00:32:45.370 --> 00:32:50.820
of America is
a thing.

00:32:50.820 --> 00:32:55.169
But it's kind of defined by a spatial region.

00:32:55.169 --> 00:32:58.380
And it's not really a kind of-- the landmass
upon which it's built,

00:32:58.380 --> 00:33:01.140
it's kind of a region that's contained within
that.

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:03.210
Anyway, I'm getting off [INAUDIBLE] topic.

00:33:03.210 --> 00:33:05.190
So you have continuance and you have occurrence.

00:33:05.190 --> 00:33:08.399
And occurrence are things like processes.

00:33:08.399 --> 00:33:13.440
So these can be
thought of as things because we can describe

00:33:13.440 --> 00:33:15.120
them with words, they are concepts.

00:33:15.120 --> 00:33:18.299
But they are not physical
entities in the same way that continuance

00:33:18.299 --> 00:33:19.559
are.

00:33:19.559 --> 00:33:21.070
So I probably just mangled.

00:33:21.070 --> 00:33:24.850
And any ontologists on the call are probably
tearing their hair out.

00:33:24.850 --> 00:33:27.010
But this is-- I just wanted to kind of include
this

00:33:27.010 --> 00:33:34.950
as a significant part of the process of working
in biomedical, in the biomedical area because

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:37.309
a lot of people use
Basic Formal Ontology.

00:33:37.309 --> 00:33:43.340
And you should go away and look at it, look
at this website, and become familiar with

00:33:43.340 --> 00:33:46.270
it if
you want to do work on this area.

00:33:46.270 --> 00:33:50.870
Another significant tool, another significant
approach is the BioPortal, which is put together

00:33:50.870 --> 00:33:53.659
by Mark Musen's
group in Stanford.

00:33:53.659 --> 00:34:01.710
And it's a large-scale knowledge--- sorry,
ontology catalog.

00:34:01.710 --> 00:34:05.559
So it's a list of various different
schema describing various different domains

00:34:05.559 --> 00:34:08.359
of knowledge.

00:34:08.359 --> 00:34:12.950
And it contains 658 ontologies, so a sizable,
sizably

00:34:12.950 --> 00:34:16.969
more than the OBO Foundry Onto-- collection.

00:34:16.969 --> 00:34:18.569
But they're pretty non-standardized.

00:34:18.569 --> 00:34:22.450
It's like anyone who can submit something
to BioPortal is allowed to do so.

00:34:22.450 --> 00:34:29.349
And so these are usually-- they're not
kind of built with the same unifying principles

00:34:29.349 --> 00:34:34.159
and constraints of having to conform to a
standard complicated,

00:34:34.159 --> 00:34:36.869
difficult ontological design.

00:34:36.869 --> 00:34:43.739
Because you can-- as you might be aware, if
you try to use the Basic Formal Ontology

00:34:43.739 --> 00:34:50.359
as the kind of core representation of your
work, you can fit it into the schema of everything

00:34:50.359 --> 00:34:54.209
else, but you also
increase your overhead in terms of being able

00:34:54.209 --> 00:34:57.109
to develop things in the short term.

00:34:57.109 --> 00:35:03.249
So BioPortal provides a nice
catalog of a large number of different representations

00:35:03.249 --> 00:35:09.319
where you can use [INAUDIBLE] kind of get
into very quickly

00:35:09.319 --> 00:35:14.530
without the overhead of having to kind of
fit it into this complex high level schema.

00:35:14.530 --> 00:35:20.690
And then, finally, I wanted to talk about
the FAIRsharing.org community.

00:35:20.690 --> 00:35:26.239
And FAIR is a kind of acronym that is
being adopted widely throughout the community

00:35:26.239 --> 00:35:29.819
to try and make sure that we, when we develop
our systems and

00:35:29.819 --> 00:35:34.029
our schema and our knowledge base and all
of these various different things, we want

00:35:34.029 --> 00:35:36.829
to-- it stands for findable,
accessible, interoperable, and reusable.

00:35:36.829 --> 00:35:41.349
So and there are a whole set of principles
that are published that one can

00:35:41.349 --> 00:35:47.200
find online-- I don't think I have a link
here, I'm afraid, I apologize for that-- that

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:51.690
allows you to, that gives you a
checklist of things you need to do in order

00:35:51.690 --> 00:35:57.190
to make your tools and systems compatible
with these principles.

00:35:57.190 --> 00:35:59.729
And
Jack, actually, in a moment of brilliance,

00:35:59.729 --> 00:36:05.259
kind of suggested that we add the letter E
on FAIR for education.

00:36:05.259 --> 00:36:09.250
You
know, because I think that's important because

00:36:09.250 --> 00:36:17.619
the-- a lot of these systems are quite challenging
to understand.

00:36:17.619 --> 00:36:22.630
And we're so busy a lot of the time kind of
dealing with computational interfaces systems

00:36:22.630 --> 00:36:25.849
and such like that we
forget that we need to actually make humans

00:36:25.849 --> 00:36:28.200
be able to use these things.

00:36:28.200 --> 00:36:34.789
Another thing about the FAIR sharing system
is that they talk about, they talk about standards,

00:36:34.789 --> 00:36:37.660
but they also talk
about kind of standards within the community's

00:36:37.660 --> 00:36:38.849
policy.

00:36:38.849 --> 00:36:40.670
So as a repository describing a large number
of these

00:36:40.670 --> 00:36:45.219
various different artifacts and things that
I was looking at, this is a good place to

00:36:45.219 --> 00:36:46.219
look.

00:36:46.219 --> 00:36:49.190
And this is a good place to
find interesting material.

00:36:49.190 --> 00:36:57.359
Now, no talk about knowledge engineering and
the web and everything would be complete without

00:36:57.359 --> 00:37:00.390
talking at least
a little bit about linked open data and the

00:37:00.390 --> 00:37:02.200
linked data cloud.

00:37:02.200 --> 00:37:09.809
Now, this is-- this is a diagram taken from
the

00:37:09.809 --> 00:37:13.609
lod.cloud.net website.

00:37:13.609 --> 00:37:21.950
And it's this gargantuan image of all of the
various different parts of the semantic web

00:37:21.950 --> 00:37:27.309
community that deals with various different
types of knowledge.

00:37:27.309 --> 00:37:29.599
And the important thing about this is that,
so if you

00:37:29.599 --> 00:37:34.529
just take a look at this, the diagram as a
whole, you'll notice that the various different

00:37:34.529 --> 00:37:38.259
parts of the diagram can be
split up into different sections.

00:37:38.259 --> 00:37:48.029
And, in fact, the life sciences forms probably
the largest individual subcommunity of

00:37:48.029 --> 00:37:50.220
the linked data cloud.

00:37:50.220 --> 00:37:59.099
And essentially, every dot in this diagram,
every circle describes a large-scale knowledge

00:37:59.099 --> 00:38:01.240
resource, a database if
you like.

00:38:01.240 --> 00:38:06.729
And the promise of what linked data can do
is that it allows you to actually connect

00:38:06.729 --> 00:38:11.559
entities from one
ontology, from one knowledge base into other

00:38:11.559 --> 00:38:14.430
common knowledge bases using ontologies.

00:38:14.430 --> 00:38:15.720
And so this is kind of
inspiring.

00:38:15.720 --> 00:38:21.369
If you just kind of sit back and look at this,
I mean, we're not going to end the details

00:38:21.369 --> 00:38:26.459
of any individual node, but
this is, this kind of gives you an idea of

00:38:26.459 --> 00:38:28.769
what could be possible.

00:38:28.769 --> 00:38:34.789
This is some stuff of human knowledge that's
available in this format currently.

00:38:34.789 --> 00:38:39.459
And if you think about every individual database
links together and how it could

00:38:39.459 --> 00:38:42.740
be used to inform things, that's actually
pretty cool.

00:38:42.740 --> 00:38:47.359
Of course, the devil is really in the details
with this stuff.

00:38:47.359 --> 00:38:50.780
And you have to be able to understand the
schemas of

00:38:50.780 --> 00:38:53.519
these various ontologies to be able to query
them effectively.

00:38:53.519 --> 00:38:56.740
And so it's not necessarily just an easy task
to pull

00:38:56.740 --> 00:39:01.140
information necessarily out of this cloud
whenever you can.

00:39:01.140 --> 00:39:02.869
But this is an expanding research area.

00:39:02.869 --> 00:39:04.380
I think it's
pretty cool.

00:39:04.380 --> 00:39:08.890
So other important stuff that I don't really
have time to talk about from the community

00:39:08.890 --> 00:39:15.170
are things like, well,
research objects.

00:39:15.170 --> 00:39:21.959
This is work by [INAUDIBLE] group when they
define-- in Manchester-- and they define,

00:39:21.959 --> 00:39:24.950
well,
these encapsulated packages of scientific

00:39:24.950 --> 00:39:25.950
knowledge.

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:29.510
So in the same way that scientific publication
kind of draws

00:39:29.510 --> 00:39:35.630
together different elements into a single
place, a research object provides a semantic

00:39:35.630 --> 00:39:39.999
web-based representation
of everything that you would need to know

00:39:39.999 --> 00:39:43.359
about a data set, including the data set itself,
problems about where

00:39:43.359 --> 00:39:49.190
the data set came from, maybe even a work
cloud that generates it, maybe even the publication

00:39:49.190 --> 00:39:51.719
that was included
in these things, So I'd recommend looking

00:39:51.719 --> 00:39:55.630
at that as an important aspect and an important
development in the

00:39:55.630 --> 00:39:56.630
field.

00:39:56.630 --> 00:40:03.799
And then, of course, web flows are a [INAUDIBLE]
longstanding piece of research in the community

00:40:03.799 --> 00:40:06.230
where,
essentially, you're basically putting computational

00:40:06.230 --> 00:40:10.619
analysis together in order to generate, in
order to be able to run

00:40:10.619 --> 00:40:14.470
programs reproducibly and reliably.

00:40:14.470 --> 00:40:17.519
But we don't talk about that anymore.

00:40:17.519 --> 00:40:19.039
So let's move on.

00:40:19.039 --> 00:40:25.869
So this-- in the next part, I wanted to--
we talked a lot about how knowledge schemas

00:40:25.869 --> 00:40:30.910
and databases and the
underlying architecture of the systems work.

00:40:30.910 --> 00:40:33.440
But it's important to leave with the notion
that schemas are not the

00:40:33.440 --> 00:40:38.660
whole story, especially when you're dealing
with the kind of representation of what knowledge

00:40:38.660 --> 00:40:40.539
is.

00:40:40.539 --> 00:40:43.589
If you look at
this-- so this figure shows you-- it's kind

00:40:43.589 --> 00:40:46.550
of a high level view of 80,000.

00:40:46.550 --> 00:40:53.849
Each dot represents an NIH grant that was
funded, I think, in 2010.

00:40:53.849 --> 00:40:58.009
I could be wrong about the exact year.

00:40:58.009 --> 00:41:00.789
And so there are 80,000 dots for each grant
that

00:41:00.789 --> 00:41:10.209
was funded by NIH, all broken down by color
for each individual agency that was, each

00:41:10.209 --> 00:41:15.819
of the agencies that were,
sorry, each institute that was funding the

00:41:15.819 --> 00:41:16.910
work.

00:41:16.910 --> 00:41:21.819
And it's important to note that most of the
databases that we're talking about here probably

00:41:21.819 --> 00:41:23.579
occur up here, right?

00:41:23.579 --> 00:41:28.390
There's all of this kind of like knowledge
and stuff that's collected into databases

00:41:28.390 --> 00:41:32.539
in areas where molecular
databases are well-founded.

00:41:32.539 --> 00:41:36.479
But I don't know of many hematological databases
that are really constructed the

00:41:36.479 --> 00:41:38.059
same level of detail.

00:41:38.059 --> 00:41:42.750
I'm not sure any epidemiology databases that
are constructed in the same way.

00:41:42.750 --> 00:41:44.539
There are
individual examples, but there's nothing like

00:41:44.539 --> 00:41:48.239
the kind of coverage that you see in molecular
biology.

00:41:48.239 --> 00:41:54.940
So, you know, just having schema, so the whole
point is that the variety of information you

00:41:54.940 --> 00:41:58.690
need to put into the
systems is very large.

00:41:58.690 --> 00:42:06.130
And when you're kind of dealing with knowledge,
you can't just talk about the structure of

00:42:06.130 --> 00:42:12.749
the knowledge itself, which is-- in this diagram,
this is a kind of three level representation

00:42:12.749 --> 00:42:19.049
of an approach called
COMMONKADS to modeling-- to modeling knowledge

00:42:19.049 --> 00:42:21.390
in a knowledge engineering context.

00:42:21.390 --> 00:42:25.019
And what's interesting about this approach
is they have a whole methodology for trying

00:42:25.019 --> 00:42:27.230
to understand the context
of the information.

00:42:27.230 --> 00:42:28.969
Like, what's the organization?

00:42:28.969 --> 00:42:32.969
How does the lab work that your information
is contained within?

00:42:32.969 --> 00:42:34.999
What is the task that the information is trying
to describe?

00:42:34.999 --> 00:42:36.809
What are the people?

00:42:36.809 --> 00:42:41.539
What are the agents involved in doing the
work?

00:42:41.539 --> 00:42:44.640
And then, you know, the actual
representation of the knowledge itself occurs

00:42:44.640 --> 00:42:45.640
here.

00:42:45.640 --> 00:42:48.089
But it is required communication models, kind
of figure out,

00:42:48.089 --> 00:42:56.009
OK, how does that knowledge-- how is that
knowledge used by the agents trying to solve

00:42:56.009 --> 00:42:59.579
a problem that the
knowledge is useful for in communication?

00:42:59.579 --> 00:43:05.509
And all of these various different elements
combined together in the overall representation

00:43:05.509 --> 00:43:09.079
of the design model to
give you the overall knowledge representation.

00:43:09.079 --> 00:43:12.749
And, of course, you know, if you're focused
only on building

00:43:12.749 --> 00:43:16.319
ontologies, then you're only talking about
one small piece of the puzzle.

00:43:16.319 --> 00:43:19.539
There's only one part of the overall
structure of how you have to think about them,

00:43:19.539 --> 00:43:21.589
the model approach.

00:43:21.589 --> 00:43:27.059
And then another thing that's overlooked a
lot of the time is, how do you evaluate knowledge

00:43:27.059 --> 00:43:28.989
systems?

00:43:28.989 --> 00:43:33.410
And
according to this book by Adelman and Riddle,

00:43:33.410 --> 00:43:36.509
they describe this kind of multi-layered representation
of the

00:43:36.509 --> 00:43:38.749
various different types of evaluation you
should think about.

00:43:38.749 --> 00:43:41.400
So-- and I'll just, I'll just put them all
out.

00:43:41.400 --> 00:43:43.729
I hope you
can see them.

00:43:43.729 --> 00:43:46.539
Now-- and so this is broken down into four
different parts.

00:43:46.539 --> 00:43:50.229
First of all, you should try and understand.

00:43:50.229 --> 00:43:54.519
So when you evaluate a knowledge system, you
want to evaluate the

00:43:54.519 --> 00:44:01.170
requirements or to validate the knowledge
in the system, whether or not is the way in

00:44:01.170 --> 00:44:04.099
which the system deals with
its knowledge correct?

00:44:04.099 --> 00:44:09.510
Does it behave appropriately?

00:44:09.510 --> 00:44:14.489
Very importantly, you know, when you build
a website or you build a system that people

00:44:14.489 --> 00:44:17.430
are going to use, can you
learn it easily?

00:44:17.430 --> 00:44:18.779
Does it work well?

00:44:18.779 --> 00:44:21.680
You have to evaluate it according to that
kind of approach.

00:44:21.680 --> 00:44:25.180
And then, finally, you
know, performance evaluation, you need to

00:44:25.180 --> 00:44:28.769
be able to say whether or not the system does
what it's supposed to

00:44:28.769 --> 00:44:30.609
do well.

00:44:30.609 --> 00:44:37.160
And so these are all kind of evaluation metrics
that are incredibly important for people doing

00:44:37.160 --> 00:44:39.599
the work that
I'm talking about.

00:44:39.599 --> 00:44:45.969
But if we're focused only on schema and ontologies
and building these artifacts, we will miss

00:44:45.969 --> 00:44:46.969
this.

00:44:46.969 --> 00:44:50.680
And so, you know, at the end of the day, it's
important to kind of-- it's probably more

00:44:50.680 --> 00:44:55.589
impactful to build
something that's less well-formed ontologically

00:44:55.589 --> 00:44:58.130
and more useful for scientists.

00:44:58.130 --> 00:45:04.140
And that's borne out by the example of a gene
ontology, which is the most widely used ontology

00:45:04.140 --> 00:45:05.140
in the world
today.

00:45:05.140 --> 00:45:06.789
It's incredibly successful.

00:45:06.789 --> 00:45:09.200
And it's incredibly useful and important.

00:45:09.200 --> 00:45:16.190
And yet, from a kind of classically,
philosophically-driven perspective, it doesn't

00:45:16.190 --> 00:45:19.309
really kind of solve-- it has-- it makes lots
of short cuts.

00:45:19.309 --> 00:45:23.259
And it takes
and it kind of solves the problem in an ad

00:45:23.259 --> 00:45:24.880
hoc way.

00:45:24.880 --> 00:45:26.910
But it does so very well.

00:45:26.910 --> 00:45:30.699
And part of the reason why it's useful
is because people can understand.

00:45:30.699 --> 00:45:41.970
And so just, OK, so I wanted to kind of jump
to-- so that kind of concludes the kind of

00:45:41.970 --> 00:45:43.680
advice giving part of the talk.

00:45:43.680 --> 00:45:46.869
We've got about five minutes left as far as
I can see.

00:45:46.869 --> 00:45:48.670
So I'll skim through the rest of it.

00:45:48.670 --> 00:45:53.279
And here I wanted to talk
about some high level ideas and kind of future

00:45:53.279 --> 00:45:56.720
directions of what we should be kind of thinking
about.

00:45:56.720 --> 00:46:02.549
And the work of Thomas Kuhn is an interesting
guide in this process.

00:46:02.549 --> 00:46:06.569
Because Thomas Kuhn kind of described the
notion of scientific paradigms.

00:46:06.569 --> 00:46:12.259
And his kind of notion is that it is basically
some accepted examples of scientific

00:46:12.259 --> 00:46:17.219
practice-- and I think of this as almost like
a community of various different elements,

00:46:17.219 --> 00:46:20.690
which includes laws,
theories, applications, instrumentations,

00:46:20.690 --> 00:46:25.299
social models of how scientists work together
within a specific community,

00:46:25.299 --> 00:46:30.699
and the coherent tradition of scientific research
that constructs a paradigm can be thought

00:46:30.699 --> 00:46:36.410
of as everything that
contributes to how paradigm works.

00:46:36.410 --> 00:46:44.140
And Kuhn in his seminal work, Structure of
Scientific Revolution talks about how normal

00:46:44.140 --> 00:46:47.719
science-- there is this kind
of big cycle that works where you're going

00:46:47.719 --> 00:46:50.609
along in normal science and then you find
something that's wrong.

00:46:50.609 --> 00:46:52.549
You
find an anomaly.

00:46:52.549 --> 00:46:55.480
And then the anomaly is so great that perhaps
you enter into crisis.

00:46:55.480 --> 00:46:57.640
And your faith in your
paradigm is shaken.

00:46:57.640 --> 00:47:02.390
And you stop believing that, you know, classical
physics works.

00:47:02.390 --> 00:47:06.099
And you have to invent an
entire new approach.

00:47:06.099 --> 00:47:07.239
You create quantum mechanics.

00:47:07.239 --> 00:47:09.029
You go through the [INAUDIBLE].

00:47:09.029 --> 00:47:12.099
And then you have to
kind of figure out how this new paradigm works

00:47:12.099 --> 00:47:13.099
within the context of neuroscience.

00:47:13.099 --> 00:47:17.200
And it's important to note that this very
rarely happens.

00:47:17.200 --> 00:47:21.329
The kind of normal scientific work that we
do day to day,

00:47:21.329 --> 00:47:26.670
the kinds of earth-shattering anomalies that
form the basis of this notion of scientific

00:47:26.670 --> 00:47:28.920
revolution very rarely
happens.

00:47:28.920 --> 00:47:33.309
And instead, we kind of find ourselves very
much in the normal science process where it's

00:47:33.309 --> 00:47:34.309
puzzle
solving.

00:47:34.309 --> 00:47:41.849
Now, so within-- and it's important to note
that there are many different paradigms all

00:47:41.849 --> 00:47:46.369
kind of over
overlapping and-- and working together that

00:47:46.369 --> 00:47:47.779
do not necessarily talk to each other.

00:47:47.779 --> 00:47:49.839
There's a kind of silo effect.

00:47:49.839 --> 00:47:53.480
So if you work in molecular biology and you
work on, let's say that you work on the molecular

00:47:53.480 --> 00:47:58.400
biology of
schizophrenia, for example, you'll look at

00:47:58.400 --> 00:48:02.069
pathways, you'll look at proteins, you'll
look at molecules, but you won't

00:48:02.069 --> 00:48:03.380
necessarily look at patients.

00:48:03.380 --> 00:48:06.520
You'll read the literature about patients,
but you don't understand how it works.

00:48:06.520 --> 00:48:10.579
So, in
a way, one can think of these-- there are

00:48:10.579 --> 00:48:15.840
two paradigms kind of working together in
a coordinated way, but they

00:48:15.840 --> 00:48:17.079
don't necessarily communicate.

00:48:17.079 --> 00:48:21.569
And part of the work that I'm trying to do
is to figure out how we-- how more easily

00:48:21.569 --> 00:48:25.640
can we get these paradigms to
kind of work together.

00:48:25.640 --> 00:48:30.690
So I'm kind of getting off the subject a little
bit, but instead of talking about this large

00:48:30.690 --> 00:48:34.619
scale
paradigm shift pattern approach a better model

00:48:34.619 --> 00:48:40.489
is treating scientific knowledge evolution
as a kind of abductive

00:48:40.489 --> 00:48:42.670
argumentation process after [INAUDIBLE].

00:48:42.670 --> 00:48:50.400
And an even better-known model, again, by
the luminary Carole Goble is this notion of

00:48:50.400 --> 00:48:51.400
knowledge turns.

00:48:51.400 --> 00:48:54.259
And a
knowledge turn is a bit like, is kind of described

00:48:54.259 --> 00:48:58.410
in this approach by this notion of the cycle
of scientific

00:48:58.410 --> 00:49:03.400
investigation, the idea that the scientists
start off with scientific knowledge, they

00:49:03.400 --> 00:49:08.959
then ask scientists questions, they
go from being-- given a question, they design

00:49:08.959 --> 00:49:12.360
experiments, they then execute experiments
to get data, and they

00:49:12.360 --> 00:49:13.360
get knowledge.

00:49:13.360 --> 00:49:17.170
And essentially, what we're trying to do is
to provide a framework to speed up the process

00:49:17.170 --> 00:49:19.440
of going
around the cycle.

00:49:19.440 --> 00:49:25.529
I am actually running out of time, so I'm
going to just quickly skim over this work.

00:49:25.529 --> 00:49:26.950
This is actually my contribution.

00:49:26.950 --> 00:49:30.680
This is something called knowledge engineering
from experimental design.

00:49:30.680 --> 00:49:38.420
The idea is that if you-- one way of
dealing with knowledge in a-- a principled

00:49:38.420 --> 00:49:42.099
way is to draw out the scientific protocol
of whatever experiment you're

00:49:42.099 --> 00:49:43.779
talking about.

00:49:43.779 --> 00:49:51.579
You can then trace the prevalence back through
the protocols indicated by this red line from

00:49:51.579 --> 00:49:54.849
the
kind of starting point in the experiment.

00:49:54.849 --> 00:50:01.920
And if you look at these various different--
the idea is that each square block is an entity.

00:50:01.920 --> 00:50:05.220
Each circle is a process.

00:50:05.220 --> 00:50:15.019
And they're variously parameterized by these
kind of blocks such as animal number, [INAUDIBLE],

00:50:15.019 --> 00:50:16.019
such like.

00:50:16.019 --> 00:50:18.829
If
you trace back the protocol in this way, you

00:50:18.829 --> 00:50:21.779
then can build a knowledge representation
that's very generic and

00:50:21.779 --> 00:50:23.489
works very well across different methods.

00:50:23.489 --> 00:50:25.479
I won't go into it.

00:50:25.479 --> 00:50:28.109
If you're interested, please look at this
paper from

00:50:28.109 --> 00:50:29.109
Ross, 2011.

00:50:29.109 --> 00:50:30.400
That should let you know.

00:50:30.400 --> 00:50:34.479
And the idea is the kind of take-home of this
is that if you approach the

00:50:34.479 --> 00:50:39.189
methodology of modeling experiments using
this kind of technique, you should be able

00:50:39.189 --> 00:50:45.249
to actually go beyond the
molecular biology experiment and try and represent

00:50:45.249 --> 00:50:47.680
data from all sorts of different things.

00:50:47.680 --> 00:50:55.329
OK, and then finally, just to kind of-- I'll
finish on a kind of high note talking about

00:50:55.329 --> 00:50:57.099
artificial scientific intelligence.

00:50:57.099 --> 00:50:59.949
Because we talk about AI all the time.

00:50:59.949 --> 00:51:04.739
And I wanted to highlight the work of Ross
King who is one of my heroes.

00:51:04.739 --> 00:51:05.839
He's awesome.

00:51:05.839 --> 00:51:07.650
He works in Manchester University.

00:51:07.650 --> 00:51:12.789
And he's built, he's spent his career building
robots that kind of go around the cycle of

00:51:12.789 --> 00:51:15.750
scientific investigation
automatically without humans.

00:51:15.750 --> 00:51:19.630
This is a robot that can reason scientifically,
construct its own theories, it comes up

00:51:19.630 --> 00:51:25.170
with its own hypotheses, and it tests those
hypotheses through experiment.

00:51:25.170 --> 00:51:28.309
It's an absolute, you know, it's really,
really interesting.

00:51:28.309 --> 00:51:32.729
Now, the thing about this is, though, it takes
a lot of-- it's a kind of a preprogrammed

00:51:32.729 --> 00:51:33.729
environment.

00:51:33.729 --> 00:51:36.180
The robot can
only ask one kind of question that it's programmed

00:51:36.180 --> 00:51:37.450
to look at.

00:51:37.450 --> 00:51:39.650
It can't think independently.

00:51:39.650 --> 00:51:45.720
And so one kind of big
high-level idea I have is, could we use educational

00:51:45.720 --> 00:51:49.859
methods that we normally score scientific
expertise in the

00:51:49.859 --> 00:51:50.900
high-level idea I have is, could we use educational
methods that we normally score scientific

00:51:50.900 --> 00:51:52.829
expertise in the
subdiscipline, could we apply that to computers

00:51:52.829 --> 00:51:55.309
and to our computers computer systems?

00:51:55.309 --> 00:52:01.729
And a colleague of ours, Rochelle Trachtenberg,
has put together a model of, in this case,

00:52:01.729 --> 00:52:07.809
statistical literacy that
rates people on levels of beginner, functional,

00:52:07.809 --> 00:52:12.049
skilled, independent, or master or expert.

00:52:12.049 --> 00:52:14.119
And I thought it would be
interesting, you know, why don't we apply

00:52:14.119 --> 00:52:15.989
this to our computational systems?

00:52:15.989 --> 00:52:20.239
If we were to apply this to our
computational systems, the very best systems

00:52:20.239 --> 00:52:23.910
that we currently have would be at this level,
beginners, where they

00:52:23.910 --> 00:52:24.980
basically remember facts.

00:52:24.980 --> 00:52:28.640
You type in a query, and it tells you what
[INAUDIBLE] remember.

00:52:28.640 --> 00:52:34.279
What would it take for us to develop computer
systems that are capable of higher levels

00:52:34.279 --> 00:52:39.039
of reasoning and
intelligence to do with science?

00:52:39.039 --> 00:52:42.410
And that's what I want to leave you with as
a final note.

00:52:42.410 --> 00:52:46.069
So thanks to-- this is just a
list of people who have inspired me and helped

00:52:46.069 --> 00:52:49.559
me and kind of given terrific leadership and
feedback in my

00:52:49.559 --> 00:52:50.589
career.

00:52:50.589 --> 00:52:54.979
And, yeah, thank you for your attention.

00:52:54.979 --> 00:52:59.789
Thank you so much, Gully, that was a really
great overview of scientific knowledge engineering

00:52:59.789 --> 00:53:03.199
and a number of
different key aspects that you need to think

00:53:03.199 --> 00:53:09.049
about as you undertake this maybe yourselves
or you're interacting

00:53:09.049 --> 00:53:12.980
with systems and kind of giving some insight
into how they're built.

00:53:12.980 --> 00:53:17.249
One question that I would like to just remind
everybody is that if you do have questions,

00:53:17.249 --> 00:53:21.849
please send them in via the questions submission
system so that we'll

00:53:21.849 --> 00:53:24.170
be able to get them in front of Dr. Burns.

00:53:24.170 --> 00:53:30.930
Gully, as people begin to consider ontologies
and how the thing about

00:53:30.930 --> 00:53:37.559
knowledge or the knowledge domain in which
they want to begin modeling, how do you, how

00:53:37.559 --> 00:53:40.069
did they begin and
how do they constrain it?

00:53:40.069 --> 00:53:46.410
I mean, it seems like you're sort of throwing
a dart in a dark room, throwing a dart at

00:53:46.410 --> 00:53:50.089
a dart board hoping you hit
some place and then that's the place that

00:53:50.089 --> 00:53:53.500
you begin and then things start to spider
outwards in terms of what

00:53:53.500 --> 00:53:57.450
you're attempting to knowledge or to knowledge
model.

00:53:57.450 --> 00:54:00.999
And then how do you stop it so that it doesn't
grow

00:54:00.999 --> 00:54:04.380
unconstrained and it becomes this intractable
problem?

00:54:04.380 --> 00:54:07.119
How do you kind of keep it real, if that makes
any sense?

00:54:07.119 --> 00:54:09.359
Yeah, OK, great, great question, Jack.

00:54:09.359 --> 00:54:20.329
So I think a good starting point is to look
at, actually, go to the big data U

00:54:20.329 --> 00:54:25.990
resource and look for courses on data modeling
generally.

00:54:25.990 --> 00:54:29.709
Now, and essentially there are tons of courses
that you

00:54:29.709 --> 00:54:37.979
can see about this kind of thing that describe,
you know, how do you develop models in the

00:54:37.979 --> 00:54:42.539
UML language, for
example, the Unified Modeling Language which

00:54:42.539 --> 00:54:46.269
is used by software engineers to develop things
like Java libraries

00:54:46.269 --> 00:54:49.160
and databases and such like.

00:54:49.160 --> 00:54:54.369
That gives you a kind of high level way of
building schema that gives you a kind of

00:54:54.369 --> 00:54:57.359
an overview that widely used within the industry.

00:54:57.359 --> 00:55:01.509
So, you know, master's level computer scientists
can easily grasp

00:55:01.509 --> 00:55:03.400
that kind of thing.

00:55:03.400 --> 00:55:10.750
And then it's a short jump from really taking
the notion of a class-based or object-oriented

00:55:10.750 --> 00:55:14.799
schema and converting
that into an [INAUDIBLE] ontology consisting

00:55:14.799 --> 00:55:23.049
of classes and attributes and elements, right?

00:55:23.049 --> 00:55:32.510
So and I think that,
you know, a colleague of mine once said, no

00:55:32.510 --> 00:55:37.940
representation without implementation as a
kind of general rule, you

00:55:37.940 --> 00:55:42.630
know, alluding to, of course, no taxation
without representation.

00:55:42.630 --> 00:55:44.529
But I think that that's a really good idea.

00:55:44.529 --> 00:55:52.660
So don't try and represent something that
doesn't go in your system at some point, you

00:55:52.660 --> 00:55:53.829
know?

00:55:53.829 --> 00:55:55.549
Don't try and
represent something that you don't have an

00:55:55.549 --> 00:55:56.819
example for.

00:55:56.819 --> 00:56:01.680
What you need to do whenever you represent
data or

00:56:01.680 --> 00:56:06.939
knowledge, what does it look like if you take
a real worked example and kind of flesh it

00:56:06.939 --> 00:56:07.939
out?

00:56:07.939 --> 00:56:10.950
And so my advice is
that you basically get a pen and paper and

00:56:10.950 --> 00:56:15.293
draw out whatever full scale example, knowledge
that you're trying to

00:56:15.293 --> 00:56:18.670
work from.

00:56:18.670 --> 00:56:22.930
And take a look at that and make sure that
that's queryable and operable so that you

00:56:22.930 --> 00:56:24.589
can actually do
stuff with it.

00:56:24.589 --> 00:56:32.279
How does knowledge engineering take into account
a kind of counter-examples, if you will?

00:56:32.279 --> 00:56:36.180
And I'm thinking of,
you know, the ISA and HASA relationships of

00:56:36.180 --> 00:56:40.040
these, the tree the classic tree diagrams
that ontologies have.

00:56:40.040 --> 00:56:42.309
And,
you know, if you're modeling something that's,

00:56:42.309 --> 00:56:49.269
you know, a human is-- has arms, you know,
and, but there are

00:56:49.269 --> 00:56:51.219
humans that don't have arms.

00:56:51.219 --> 00:56:58.309
And so how can one kind of keep track of alternatives
within an ontological system

00:56:58.309 --> 00:57:02.029
that still kind of, you know, they're still
human, but this person was born without arms

00:57:02.029 --> 00:57:06.440
or had them, you know, they
were lost in an accident or something.

00:57:06.440 --> 00:57:08.299
How does it handle that sort of stuff?

00:57:08.299 --> 00:57:09.299
Right.

00:57:09.299 --> 00:57:12.319
Well, I think that it's like any piece of
computational architecture.

00:57:12.319 --> 00:57:18.200
You have to-- the more complex and rigid
you make-- the more complex and rigid you

00:57:18.200 --> 00:57:20.739
make it, the less likely it is to be useful.

00:57:20.739 --> 00:57:26.059
And yet the more easily, the
more quick and dirty you build it, you'll

00:57:26.059 --> 00:57:29.689
be able to get it up and running, but it won't
be able to solve these

00:57:29.689 --> 00:57:32.729
individual cases.

00:57:32.729 --> 00:57:37.160
I think that, again, I go back to this idea
of, what do you want this representation of

00:57:37.160 --> 00:57:39.819
a person with arms for?

00:57:39.819 --> 00:57:40.890
Is it a
patient intake form?

00:57:40.890 --> 00:57:48.719
Is it a representation, you know, what's the
actual use case that the knowledge is going

00:57:48.719 --> 00:57:50.269
to be
used for?

00:57:50.269 --> 00:57:54.469
And then you can do an error analysis and
say, OK, look of all the people we're going

00:57:54.469 --> 00:57:56.180
to see, this is not
an important set of information.

00:57:56.180 --> 00:58:01.640
But-- and, of course, that isn't a kind of
universal solution that solves the problem

00:58:01.640 --> 00:58:03.890
from a AI complete kind of point of view.

00:58:03.890 --> 00:58:07.049
But it's still, that's pretty much my approach.

00:58:07.049 --> 00:58:14.529
I haven't talked about data modeling in a--
using statistical models and neural networks

00:58:14.529 --> 00:58:15.529
in this talk.

00:58:15.529 --> 00:58:16.529
Because
we're--

00:58:16.529 --> 00:58:20.660
That was going to be my next question is,
can some--

00:58:20.660 --> 00:58:21.660
Yeah.

00:58:21.660 --> 00:58:23.969
---relationships be probabilistic, for example?

00:58:23.969 --> 00:58:24.969
Or--
Yeah.

00:58:24.969 --> 00:58:26.670
--yeah, I don't know.

00:58:26.670 --> 00:58:29.210
I was going to ask you about that.

00:58:29.210 --> 00:58:32.160
Yeah, that's a whole.

00:58:32.160 --> 00:58:36.329
That's another talk I think that
there's-- you know, when we're talking about

00:58:36.329 --> 00:58:42.199
biomedical databases, as you can see, right,
the databases that

00:58:42.199 --> 00:58:49.119
people use in the scientific community are
still mostly working with knowledge at the

00:58:49.119 --> 00:58:53.410
level of tables and attributes
and schema.

00:58:53.410 --> 00:58:55.269
Right, right.

00:58:55.269 --> 00:59:03.859
But there is a big push to try and build these
systems that are capable of reasoning over

00:59:03.859 --> 00:59:07.819
the complex data spaces
that you see in the real world like the space

00:59:07.819 --> 00:59:13.229
of all microscope images taken from brain
tissue.

00:59:13.229 --> 00:59:18.459
If you were sit down
and you kind of like look at those brain images

00:59:18.459 --> 00:59:22.089
and you try and build a taxonomy, an ISA taxonomy
of the different

00:59:22.089 --> 00:59:25.969
types of brain images, you will not succeed,
right?

00:59:25.969 --> 00:59:28.960
But if you try to classify-- I've seen work
where people are

00:59:28.960 --> 00:59:36.619
trying to classify it who are able to leverage,
who are able to classify tumors or various

00:59:36.619 --> 00:59:44.079
different kinds of growth in
the brain and such like, but will also, and

00:59:44.079 --> 00:59:52.589
will also be able to kind of, yeah, do other
interesting things with the

00:59:52.589 --> 00:59:53.589
data.

00:59:53.589 --> 01:00:04.019
So neural networks is very much a, very much
a kind of an important and exciting framework

01:00:04.019 --> 01:00:08.440
for things to kind of
move forward with.

01:00:08.440 --> 01:00:13.119
Just in general, I think that's an observation
that you see in literature.

01:00:13.119 --> 01:00:17.689
This is definitely kind of
the trend of [INAUDIBLE], probably the most

01:00:17.689 --> 01:00:20.979
exciting trend that I've seen in terms of
where this is going to go.

01:00:20.979 --> 01:00:23.631
It's
not obvious exactly how it fits in with the

01:00:23.631 --> 01:00:28.059
existing symbolic representation of things
as a general open-ended

01:00:28.059 --> 01:00:36.239
question, but, you know, that's-- I think
that's a very exciting way in which this field

01:00:36.239 --> 01:00:37.329
has developed.

01:00:37.329 --> 01:00:39.439
Well that may very well be the future.

01:00:39.439 --> 01:00:41.809
Well, we've reached the top of the hour, everyone.

01:00:41.809 --> 01:00:45.380
Thank you so much for
taking part in the Big Data to Knowledge Guide

01:00:45.380 --> 01:00:47.150
of the Fundamentals of Data Science.

01:00:47.150 --> 01:00:51.670
Thank you to Gully Burns
for sharing his thoughts on scientific knowledge

01:00:51.670 --> 01:00:53.049
engineering with us.

01:00:53.049 --> 01:00:54.969
We really appreciate it, Gully.

01:00:54.969 --> 01:01:00.969
And keep your eyes peeled in your email for
